il recently, the argument was often framed as economic development versus local resistance. The new audit acknowledges a third constituency: electricity customers who may never live near a server campus but could still be affected by grid costs. If utilities build substations and transmission lines for a massive customer that later scales back, regulators must decide who absorbs the stranded expense. State leaders increasingly say that risk should not automatically be shifted to households and small businesses.","Data center companies argue that the industry can strengthen Texas if rules are predictable. Large campuses can sign long-term power contracts, support new generation, provide flexible load during emergencies and create a market for advanced energy projects. Some operators are also planning private generation or batteries that reduce dependence on the public grid. The problem is that those benefits vary sharply from project to project. An audit can help regulators distinguish a facility that arrives with firm power arrangements from one that assumes the grid will solve every problem after the site is announced.","The review also arrives as lawmakers debate whether the Public Utility Commission and ERCOT have enough legal authority to manage enormous new loads. Grid operators were designed primarily to connect power plants and traditional customers, not thousands of megawatts of computing demand appearing in a few years. The Texas Legislature may ultimately need to define deposit requirements, construction milestones, cost-sharing rules and penalties for projects that reserve capacity without building. The audit can identify the weaknesses, but durable policy will likely require legislation.","For communities, the best outcome is not necessarily fewer data centers. It is a system in which projects that move forward are financially credible and pay a fair share of the infrastructure they require. A serious applicant should be able to explain how much electric