combination of a high-profile former legislator, a high-paying state position and a sparse public job description is enough to justify continued scrutiny.","The vacancy Schatzline left behind in the House is another practical consequence. When a legislator leaves for an executive appointment, constituents lose their elected representative until the seat is filled under the state’s election process. That can matter if the vacancy overlaps with committee work, constituent services or preparations for the next session. The appointment therefore has effects beyond the governor’s staff chart.","Records requests may eventually provide a clearer picture of the role. Calendars, memoranda, correspondence and draft policy documents can show which agencies and lawmakers the adviser is working with and what proposals are being developed. Those records will be especially useful if the public job description remains general. The most reliable way to judge the position will be to follow what it produces, not merely what supporters or critics say it is supposed to do."]}],"faq":[],"sources":[{"label":"Houston Chronicle — source report","url":"https://www.houstonchronicle.com/"}],"keyTakeaways":["Former state Rep. Nate Schatzline is now one of the governor’s higher-paid advisers, but public records leave major questions about the scope of his election-policy work."]}$json$::jsonb body_json,
       $links$[{"href":"/elections","kind":"hub","label":"Texas Elections"},{"href":"/texas-news","kind":"hub","label":"Texas News"}]$links$::jsonb internal_links
)
